---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ReceiverWidget from '../components/ReceiverWidget.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Receive a Screen Share — QuiiCast"
	description="Enter a 4-digit code to receive a screen share instantly — works on iPhone, Android, and any browser. No account or download required."
	path="/receiver"
>
	<div class="text-center">
		<h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white sm:text-4xl">Watch a shared screen</h1>
		<p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-md text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
			Enter the 4-digit code shown on the caster's screen to connect and start watching instantly.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-lg">
		<ReceiverWidget client:load />
	</div>

	<section class="prose mx-auto mt-20 max-w-2xl">
		<h2>How receiving a screen share works</h2>
		<p>
			Whoever is sharing their screen starts from the <a href="/caster">cast page</a> and gets a
			random 4-digit code. Type that code into the box above and click <strong>Connect</strong> — your
			browser opens a direct, peer-to-peer connection to theirs and their screen appears on yours
			within a second or two. There's no account to create and nothing to install; if your browser
			can load this page, it can receive a screen share.
		</p>
		<p>
			Once connected, use the player controls to mute or unmute audio, pause the picture, go full
			screen, or adjust quality on your end independently of what the caster picked — useful if
			you're on a slower connection than they are. Up to 5 people can watch the same code at once,
			so a study group, a small meeting, or a family member walking through a problem can all join
			from the same 4-digit code.
		</p>

		<h2>If it's not connecting</h2>
		<p>
			Double-check the code is current — codes expire after a short period of inactivity and
			automatically refresh, so if the caster's session has been running a while, ask them to read
			out the latest code shown on their screen. A black screen that never loads usually means the
			two devices couldn't find a direct path to each other, which is more common on strict
			corporate networks or mobile carrier connections — trying again after a few seconds, or asking
			the caster to switch off "Prefer local WiFi" if you're on different networks, usually resolves it.
		</p>

		<h2>FAQ</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Do I need to sign up to watch?</strong><br />
			No — receiving a screen share never requires an account, a download, or a browser extension.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Can I watch on my phone?</strong><br />
			Yes — the receive page works in any modern mobile browser on iPhone or Android. Watching a
			screen share works on iOS even though iPhones can't be the one <em>sending</em> a share, due to
			a restriction Apple applies at the OS level.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Can the person sharing see anything on my device?</strong><br />
			No — the connection is one-way. You're watching their screen; they have no visibility into
			yours at all.
		</p>
		<p>
			See the <a href="/caster">cast page</a> if you're the one who needs to share instead of watch,
			or check the full <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> for more questions.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
